<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <!--
    WHY NOT ELEVATED — corrected 2026-08-21 (loop 512). This manifest said
    requireAdministrator from loop 21 to loop 512, on the theory that the League game runs
    elevated and UIPI would starve a lower-integrity hook of keyboard input. That theory was
    already contradicted by the project's own loop-25 measurement (elevation did NOT fix the
    RegisterHotKey failure — the WH_KEYBOARD_LL hook did), and a live test with an asInvoker
    build confirmed the hook receives input over the game without elevation: League runs at
    medium integrity, same as this app. Nothing in this app calls an API that needs admin
    (SetWindowsHookEx(WH_KEYBOARD_LL), topmost z-order, WGC capture: all unprivileged), so
    asInvoker loses nothing and drops the UAC prompt, the elevated-file-lock deploy pain, and
    the worst-optics combination of an unsigned binary demanding admin plus a keyboard hook.
    Edge case: if a user deliberately runs League elevated, UIPI then applies — run Fova
    elevated too (right-click → run as administrator). That is opt-in, not the default.

    WHAT IT ACTUALLY DOES — corrected 2026-08-21. The earlier text here said this build
    "does not hook input". That stopped being true when hotkeys moved from RegisterHotKey
    (whose WM_HOTKEY messages the focused game swallowed, even elevated) to a
    WH_KEYBOARD_LL hook, and the wording was never updated. Stating it plainly instead:

      * It DOES install a low-level keyboard hook (Hotkeys/LowLevelHotkeyHook.cs).
      * That hook NEVER swallows a key — HookCallback has exactly one return path,
        CallNextHookEx, so every keystroke reaches the game untouched.
      * It NEVER logs, stores, buffers or transmits a keystroke. The only state kept is the
        set of registered hotkey combos, which modifier and other keys are currently held
        (only while held), the Tab-scoreboard and chat open/closed flags (HotkeyGate), and
        whether the foreground window is the League game window (class/title check).
      * It does NOT use GetAsyncKeyState/GetKeyboardState, and sends NO input
        (no SendInput/keybd_event) — it cannot act on the player's behalf (P4).
      * It does NOT read game memory or inject code into any process (P3).

    In effect it is RegisterHotKey that also works over the game. If you would rather not
    run a build that installs a keyboard hook at all, the alternative implementation
    (Hotkeys/Win32HotkeyHook.cs) is still in the tree — but it loses in-game hotkeys and
    the click-to-target modifier gate that depends on IsModifierHeld.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>
  <!--
    Per-monitor DPI aware (v2), loop 565. Without it WPF is only system-DPI aware: on a monitor
    whose display scale differs from the primary's, Windows bitmap-stretches the overlay and
    virtualizes the League window's coordinates, so the overlay lands at the wrong size/place.
    With it, GetClientRect/ClientToScreen return physical pixels everywhere and each window
    renders at its own monitor's scale. dpiAware true/pm is the pre-1607 fallback.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
